# HTTPS Only in Production

In production, serve and call APIs over HTTPS only. Never bind, redirect to, or configure production endpoints with plain `http://`.

## Do / Don't

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — production over HTTP
app.listen(80);
const apiUrl = "http://api.example.com";
res.cookie("access_token", jwt, { httpOnly: true, secure: false });

// ✅ GOOD — TLS in production; secure cookies
const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === "production";
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (isProd && req.secure !== true && req.headers["x-forwarded-proto"] !== "https") {
    return res.redirect(301, `https://${req.headers.host}${req.url}`);
  }
  next();
});
const apiUrl = process.env.API_URL; // e.g. https://api.example.com
res.cookie("access_token", jwt, {
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: isProd,
  sameSite: "strict",
});
```

## Checklist

- Production public URLs, webhooks, and client `fetch`/`axios` bases use `https://`.
- Cookies that carry auth set `secure: true` in production (pairs with the JWT cookie rule).
- Terminate TLS at the reverse proxy/load balancer or use a proper HTTPS server — do not expose plain HTTP publicly.
- Localhost/dev sidecars (`http://127.0.0.1:...`) are fine for local tooling; do not ship that pattern for production hosts.
```

This fits next to your JWT rule (`secure: true` on cookies) and applies to every session once `alwaysApply: true` is set.
